    {
      "id": "S02",
      "act": 0,
      "title": null,
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "DocShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "rockyou.png",
            "quote": "rockyou-quote.png",
            "quoteStep": 1,
            "source": "TechCrunch, 14 Dec 2009",
            "focus": [
              0.5,
              0.3
            ]
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S02_01",
              "vo": "In December two thousand nine, a company called Rock You was hacked."
            },
            {
              "id": "S02_02",
              "vo": "About thirty-two million passwords leaked, and they were stored as plain, readable text."
            }
          ],
          "sfx": [
            "hit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HeapSet",
          "props": {
            "big": "123456",
            "count": 290731,
            "perCard": 1000
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S02_03",
              "vo": "The most common password was one two three four five six. Almost three hundred thousand accounts used it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "FootageShot",
          "props": {
            "file": "gpu-rtx-rgb-fans.mp4",
            "rate": 0.68,
            "tags": [
              {
                "x": 960,
                "y": 128,
                "text": "one modern graphics card",
                "at": 0.08
              },
              {
                "x": 960,
                "y": 200,
                "text": "every 8-character a–z 0–9 password",
                "at": 0.3
              },
              {
                "x": 960,
                "y": 272,
                "text": "2,821,109,907,456 guesses",
                "at": 0.56
              }
            ],
            "scrim": true
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S02_04",
              "vo": "Other sites did protect their passwords. But one modern graphics card can try every eight-character password of lowercase letters and digits, in some of those formats, in under a minute."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S03",
      "act": 1,
      "title": "1 · Keep it",
      "transitionIn": "fade",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "LottieShot",
          "props": {
            "file": "s03-signup",
            "scale": 1,
            "bg": "#F1EFE9"
          },
          "sfx": [
            "whoosh"
          ],
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S03_01",
              "vo": "Let's say you sign up for a new grocery app. Your password is mango at the rate one two three."
            },
            {
              "id": "S03_02",
              "vo": "The simplest thing the app can do is save it as it is. One column for your email, one for your password."
            },
            {
              "id": "S03_03",
              "vo": "It works. You log in. The app compares what you typed with what it saved, and lets you in."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "CatalogSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "copy",
            "field": "plain",
            "color": "#4E3A36",
            "tags": [
              {
                "x": 1290,
                "y": 110,
                "text": "the copy holds every password",
                "atStep": 0,
                "color": "#FF5A4E"
              }
            ],
            "states": [
              {
                "step": 0,
                "field": "plain",
                "pos": [
                  -1.3,
                  3.5,
                  8.2
                ],
                "posTo": [
                  -1.25,
                  3.42,
                  7.9
                ],
                "target": [
                  1.75,
                  1.15,
                  0.1
                ]
              }
            ],
            "copyLift": 0.06,
            "copyTo": [
              3.6,
              0.06,
              0
            ],
            "copyOpacity": 1
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S03_04",
              "vo": "It also means anyone who copies that table walks away with every password in it. That was Rock You."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LottieShot",
          "props": {
            "file": "s03-reuse",
            "scale": 1,
            "bg": "#F1EFE9"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S03_05",
              "vo": "And attackers try leaked passwords on other sites. If you reused yours, one leak can open those accounts too."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S04",
      "act": 1,
      "title": "Encrypt it",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "LockboxSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "lock",
            "angle": "lid",
            "plain": "mango@123",
            "cipher": "6FeRQifMiphtMzZfI0QUmw=="
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S04_01",
              "vo": "The obvious fix is to lock the column. Encrypt each password with a secret key."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "CatalogSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "drawer",
            "angle": "drawer",
            "field": "cipher"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S04_02",
              "vo": "Now the table shows scrambled text instead of mango at the rate one two three."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LockboxSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "unlock",
            "angle": "rise",
            "plain": "mango@123",
            "cipher": "6FeRQifMiphtMzZfI0QUmw=="
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S04_03",
              "vo": "But encryption is built to be undone. Whoever holds the key can turn every row back into the original."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S05",
      "act": 1,
      "title": "Adobe, 2013",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "adobe-krebs.png",
            "source": "Krebs on Security · 29 Oct 2013",
            "focus": [
              0,
              0.25
            ]
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_01",
              "vo": "In twenty thirteen, Adobe was breached."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LockboxSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "locked",
            "angle": "stamp",
            "plain": "mango@123",
            "cipher": "6FeRQifMiphtMzZfI0QUmw==",
            "stamp": "TRIPLE DES"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_02",
              "vo": "Its passwords were encrypted, not hashed, with a cipher called Triple D E S."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LockboxSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "wall",
            "angle": "wall",
            "plain": "mango@123",
            "cipher": "6FeRQifMiphtMzZfI0QUmw=="
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_03",
              "vo": "Australia's privacy regulator later found that Adobe used a single encryption key for all of them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "adobe-oaic.png",
            "quote": "adobe-oaic-quote.png",
            "source": "OAIC · Adobe investigation report · June 2015",
            "note": "Counted by Troy Hunt: ≈109M records had a hint",
            "at": 45,
            "quoteBand": [
              2672,
              213,
              57,
              161
            ]
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_04",
              "vo": "About a hundred and nine million records also had a password hint, stored in plain text."
            }
          ],
          "sfx": [
            "tick"
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "adobe-krebs.png",
            "quote": "adobe-krebs-quote.png",
            "source": "Krebs on Security · 29 Oct 2013",
            "note": "Counted by Have I Been Pwned: ≈153M records in the leaked file",
            "at": 45,
            "focus": [
              0,
              0.3
            ],
            "quoteBand": [
              1677,
              347,
              54,
              287
            ]
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_05",
              "vo": "Adobe said about thirty-eight million active users were hit. The leaked file had about a hundred and fifty-three million records."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LockboxSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "hints",
            "angle": "hints",
            "closed": true,
            "plain": "mango@123",
            "cipher": "6FeRQifMiphtMzZfI0QUmw==",
            "hints": [
              "my dog's name",
              "my dog's name",
              "my dog's name"
            ]
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_06",
              "vo": "A hint like \"my dog's name\" gives a guesser a head start."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LightTableSet",
          "props": {
            "action": "match",
            "angle": "wide",
            "a": {
              "label": "WHAT YOU TYPED",
              "hex": "81c68634d1b211e0d5632839f7efc8601c743f1ef0c94da8220e26ab221efff1"
            },
            "b": {
              "label": "WHAT IS STORED",
              "hex": "81c68634d1b211e0d5632839f7efc8601c743f1ef0c94da8220e26ab221efff1"
            }
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S05_07",
              "vo": "So encryption solved the wrong problem. The site never needed to read your password back. It only needs to check it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S06",
      "act": 2,
      "title": "2 · Fingerprint it",
      "transitionIn": "fade",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "reveal"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S06_01",
              "vo": "Checking without reading back is what a cryptographic hash does."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "fixed"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S06_02",
              "vo": "It turns any text into a fingerprint of fixed length."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "punch"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S06_03",
              "vo": "Feed it mango at the rate one two three, and SHA two fifty-six gives back sixty-four characters, a jumble of digits and letters. It looks like noise."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "reverse"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S06_04",
              "vo": "And there's no practical way to run it backwards. An attacker can still guess, hash the guess, and compare."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S07",
      "act": 2,
      "title": "The avalanche",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "CompareC",
          "props": {
            "mode": "avalanche"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S07_01",
              "vo": "Change one character, and the fingerprint changes all over."
            },
            {
              "id": "S07_02",
              "vo": "Hunter two and hunter three differ by a single digit. The differences are scattered across the whole fingerprint."
            },
            {
              "id": "S07_03",
              "vo": "In this pair, one hundred and forty-six of the two hundred and fifty-six bits flipped."
            },
            {
              "id": "S07_04",
              "vo": "So a near miss doesn't look like a near miss."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S08",
      "act": 2,
      "title": "Login, with hashes",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "UsersDrawerC",
          "props": {
            "field": "sha256"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S08_01",
              "vo": "So the app stores the fingerprint instead of the password."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "login"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S08_02",
              "vo": "When you log in, it hashes what you just typed, and compares the two fingerprints."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "CompareC",
          "props": {
            "mode": "match"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S08_03",
              "vo": "They match, so the app lets you in."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "UsersDrawerC",
          "props": {
            "field": "sha256",
            "copy": true
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S08_04",
              "vo": "If that table leaks, the attacker gets fingerprints, not passwords. That sounds airtight. LinkedIn found out it wasn't."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S09",
      "act": 2,
      "title": "LinkedIn, 2012",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceC",
          "props": {
            "year": "2012"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_01",
              "vo": "In twenty twelve, a file of about six and a half million LinkedIn password hashes was posted online."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "UsersDrawerC",
          "props": {
            "field": "sha1"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_02",
              "vo": "They were SHA one hashes, with nothing added. Unsalted."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "repeat"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_03",
              "vo": "That matters because the same password always produces the same fingerprint."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LookupC",
          "props": {
            "action": "file"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_04",
              "vo": "Attackers don't need to reverse anything. They hash millions of common passwords once, ahead of time, and keep the results in a lookup table."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LookupC",
          "props": {
            "action": "search"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_05",
              "vo": "Then they just search. If your fingerprint is in their table, your password is the row next to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceC",
          "props": {
            "year": "2016"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S09_06",
              "vo": "Four years later, LinkedIn said it believed the breach was far bigger: a hundred and seventeen million emails and passwords."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S10",
      "act": 2,
      "title": "Salt",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "clip"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S10_01",
              "vo": "The fix is called salting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "HashMachineC",
          "props": {
            "action": "salt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S10_02",
              "vo": "Each password gets its own random string, mixed in before hashing. That string is the salt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "UsersDrawerC",
          "props": {
            "field": "salted"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S10_03",
              "vo": "It isn't secret. It's stored right next to the hash. Its job is to make the same password hash differently for every user."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "CompareC",
          "props": {
            "mode": "salt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S10_04",
              "vo": "Two people who both chose mango at the rate one two three now get completely different fingerprints."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "LookupC",
          "props": {
            "action": "salt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S10_05",
              "vo": "As long as salts are long and random, a table built in advance stops working. It would need a separate table for every salt."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S11",
      "act": 3,
      "title": "3 · Slow it down",
      "transitionIn": "fade",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "DAttack",
          "props": {
            "mode": "salt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S11_01",
              "vo": "But salt only stops the shortcut. It doesn't stop guessing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DAttack",
          "props": {
            "mode": "loop"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S11_02",
              "vo": "An attacker with a leaked salt and hash can still try passwords one by one. Hash a guess with that salt, compare, repeat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DHeap",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S11_03",
              "vo": "Real people make that easier. In the Rock You leak, nearly half the passwords were names, dictionary words or simple patterns."
            },
            {
              "id": "S11_04",
              "vo": "So attackers try the likely passwords first, and the long tail later."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DAttack",
          "props": {
            "mode": "fast"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S11_05",
              "vo": "And the hashes we've seen so far are fast to calculate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S12",
      "act": 3,
      "title": "The GPU race",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "DGPUBench",
          "props": {
            "mode": "intro"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S12_01",
              "vo": "Take one gaming graphics card, an R T X forty ninety, running the cracking tool hash cat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DEvidence",
          "props": {
            "kind": "fast"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S12_02",
              "vo": "It tries about a hundred and sixty-four billion M D five guesses every second."
            },
            {
              "id": "S12_03",
              "vo": "About fifty billion for SHA one. About twenty-two billion for SHA two fifty-six."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DGPUBench",
          "props": {
            "mode": "space"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S12_04",
              "vo": "Take every possible eight-character password made of lowercase letters and digits. That's about two point eight trillion of them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DGPUBench",
          "props": {
            "mode": "times"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S12_05",
              "vo": "With M D five, that card gets through all of them in about seventeen seconds. With SHA one, under a minute. SHA two fifty-six, about two minutes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S13",
      "act": 3,
      "title": "Slow on purpose",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "DSlowMachine",
          "props": {
            "mode": "cost"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S13_01",
              "vo": "So the real fix is to make each guess expensive."
            },
            {
              "id": "S13_02",
              "vo": "Password hashing functions are built to be slow on purpose, and the slowness can be turned up as hardware gets faster."
            },
            {
              "id": "S13_03",
              "vo": "B crypt arrived in nineteen ninety-nine."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DSlowBenchmark",
          "props": {
            "algorithm": "bcrypt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S13_04",
              "vo": "On the same card, B crypt at a low setting manages about a hundred and eighty-four thousand guesses a second. That same set of passwords now takes about half a year."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DSlowMachine",
          "props": {
            "mode": "memory"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S13_05",
              "vo": "S crypt arrived in two thousand nine. It also makes each guess use a lot of memory, to blunt custom cracking hardware."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DSlowBenchmark",
          "props": {
            "algorithm": "scrypt"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S13_06",
              "vo": "At the tested setting, the card manages about seven thousand guesses a second. About twelve and a half years for the same set."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S14",
      "act": 3,
      "title": "Argon2",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "DEvidence",
          "props": {
            "kind": "owasp"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_01",
              "vo": "The Oh wasp guidance on password storage recommends Argon two I D. It comes from Argon two, which won an open password hashing competition in twenty fifteen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DSlowMachine",
          "props": {
            "mode": "argon"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_02",
              "vo": "Its minimum setting uses about twenty megabytes of memory and two rounds of work for every single guess."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DRecipe",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_03",
              "vo": "In a common library's format, the stored value carries its own recipe. One line holds the algorithm and its settings. Then the salt. Then the hash."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DRehash",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_04",
              "vo": "Because the settings travel with the hash, a site can raise the cost later. It re-hashes your password the next time you log in."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DPepper",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_05",
              "vo": "Some sites add one more layer, called a pepper. It's a single secret kept outside the database, so a stolen table alone isn't enough to start guessing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "DSlowMachine",
          "props": {
            "mode": "final"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S14_06",
              "vo": "So picture the grocery app's table leaking today. Each row gives an attacker a salt and a slow fingerprint, and every guess costs real time."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S15",
      "act": 4,
      "title": "4 · Your side",
      "transitionIn": "fade",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "ELogin",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S15_01",
              "vo": "There's one moment the site does see your password. Every time you log in."
            },
            {
              "id": "S15_02",
              "vo": "It arrives over an encrypted connection, then gets hashed and compared with the stored value."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EEmail",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S15_03",
              "vo": "So if a site ever emails you the password you forgot, it can recover your password. That's the warning sign."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S16",
      "act": 4,
      "title": "The rules changed",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "nist.png",
            "source": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · July 2025"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S16_01",
              "vo": "The current guidelines reflect all of this."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "nist.png",
            "quote": "nist-salted-hashed-quote.png",
            "source": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · July 2025",
            "at": 105,
            "quoteBand": [
              2162,
              570,
              63,
              513
            ],
            "quoteSource": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · §3.1.1.2 · July 2025"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S16_02",
              "vo": "The U S standards body Nist now requires salted password hashing in its guidelines, and recommends the highest cost a site can practically handle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "nist.png",
            "quote": "nist-no-rotation-quote.png",
            "source": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · July 2025",
            "at": 0,
            "quoteBand": [
              2082,
              226,
              4,
              163
            ],
            "quoteSource": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · §3.1.1.2 · July 2025"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S16_03",
              "vo": "Under these guidelines, sites must not force you to change your password on a schedule, like every ninety days. They must force a change if there's evidence it was compromised."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "nist.png",
            "quote": "nist-min-length-quote.png",
            "source": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · July 2025",
            "at": 0,
            "quoteBand": [
              2082,
              286,
              62,
              285
            ],
            "quoteSource": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · §3.1.1.2 · July 2025"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S16_04",
              "vo": "Sites must not demand symbols and mixed case. They ask for length instead. At least fifteen characters, if the password is your only login factor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EvidenceShot",
          "props": {
            "page": "nist.png",
            "quote": "nist-blocklist-quote.png",
            "source": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · July 2025",
            "at": 0,
            "quoteBand": [
              2162,
              284,
              63,
              228
            ],
            "quoteSource": "NIST SP 800-63B-4 · §3.1.1.2 · July 2025"
          },
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S16_05",
              "vo": "And sites must check new passwords against a blocklist of commonly used, expected or compromised passwords."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S17",
      "act": 4,
      "title": "Checking without telling",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "EHibpSource",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S17_01",
              "vo": "That last check can work without sending your password, or even its full hash."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "ECheck",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S17_02",
              "vo": "Your browser or app hashes the password with SHA one, and sends only the first five characters to the service Have I Been Poned."
            },
            {
              "id": "S17_03",
              "vo": "The service returns the endings of matching leaked hashes in its database. Your device checks its own ending against that list."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "S18",
      "act": 4,
      "title": "Putting it together",
      "transitionIn": "cut",
      "shots": [
        {
          "visual": "ERecap",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S18_01",
              "vo": "Put the whole chain together. Plain text leaks everything. Encryption leaks everything to whoever holds the key."
            },
            {
              "id": "S18_02",
              "vo": "A plain hash can fall to a lookup table. A salted fast hash can fall to a graphics card. A salted slow hash makes every guess expensive."
            },
            {
              "id": "S18_03",
              "vo": "Back to the most popular password in the Rock You leak. A sensible attacker tries the most common passwords first. So even behind Argon two, one two three four five six would be among the first guesses."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EManager",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S18_04",
              "vo": "So the site's job is to make guessing expensive. Your job is a long password you've used nowhere else, so a leak on one site can't be replayed on your other accounts."
            },
            {
              "id": "S18_05",
              "vo": "Our advice: use a password manager. Nist now requires sites to allow them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "visual": "EDrawerClose",
          "props": {},
          "beats": [
            {
              "id": "S18_06",
              "vo": "What you can't see from a login screen is how a site stores your password. That half of the job is out of your hands. Your half isn't."
            },
            {
              "id": "S18_07",
              "vo": "And if a breach notice ever says passwords were salted and hashed with something slow, you'll know what that bought you. Time."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
